dsh: reasoning:
Now I understand the structure. The D11 bridge:
- `HeatKernelCore X` — D7 interface minus `initialCondition`
- `FullInitialCondition` — the literal D7 field: all continuous f (overstrong on noncompact Euclidean space)
- `WeakInitialCondition` — continuous + integrable f
- D11 proved `flatHeatKernelCore_weakInitialCondition`: for the explicit Euclidean kernel, WeakInitialCondition holds in every dimension.

My task (D12-heat-domain-repair):
1. Repair the D7 initial-condition domain: "all continuous functions on noncompact Euclidean space is overstrong".
2. Define a versioned admissible-test-function interface (continuous compact support or an explicitly suitable integrable class).
3. Prove the D11 Euclidean core inhabits it for every dimension.
4. Prove precisely scoped compatibility for compact finite-measure settings: on compact spaces (finite measure), continuous ⇒ integrable, so FullInitialCondition ⇒ WeakInitialCondition and vice versa; more specifically for compact finite-measure settings the two conditions coincide, so the D7 literal condition is fine there.
5. Do not assume FullInitialCondition in positive dimension; do not replace genuine Laplacians by arbitrary operators.
6. Keep legacy definitions unchanged until downstream compatibility is proved.
7. If feasible formalize an explicit fast-growing counterexample to the old condition; label the informal obstruction separately until then.

